                            <comment id="502045" author="sebastien" created="Thu, 24 Sep 2026 15:30:53 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;What happens is the following.&lt;br/&gt;
The client console shows, right after a context flush:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;preformatted panel&quot; style=&quot;border-width: 1px;&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;preformattedContent panelContent&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;  gss_kt_update()) lustre-MDT0001_UUID at 10.240.43.248@tcp:
  negotiation: rpc err 0, gss err 70000
  ll_close_inode_openhandle()) mdc close failed: rc = -1
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;gss err 0x70000 is GSS_S_NO_CRED, and rpc err 0 means the&lt;br/&gt;
negotiation never reached the server: gss_init_sec_context()&lt;br/&gt;
failed on the client because no credential was available. So&lt;br/&gt;
gss_kt_update() ends up with -EACCES, flags the context with&lt;br/&gt;
PTLRPC_CTX_ERROR_BIT, and sptlrpc_req_refresh_ctx() completes the&lt;br/&gt;
request with -EPERM, which dbench reports as a failed operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The credential goes missing because of the test itself. &apos;lfs&lt;br/&gt;
flushctx -k&apos; runs kdestroy only after the ioctl that flushes the&lt;br/&gt;
contexts, so it destroys the credentials cache while the gss&lt;br/&gt;
upcalls triggered by that very flush are still running.&lt;br/&gt;
lgss_keyring validates the root TGT in lkrb5_prepare_root_cred(),&lt;br/&gt;
but gss_init_sec_context() is called later with&lt;br/&gt;
GSS_C_NO_CREDENTIAL, so the credential is only looked up in the&lt;br/&gt;
ccache once kdestroy may already have emptied it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;test_90 is meant to check that the client recovers from losing its&lt;br/&gt;
gss contexts under load, not from losing its Kerberos credentials,&lt;br/&gt;
so we should just drop -k.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
